      {
        "id": "p31-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that the points $(3,-2)$, $(7,6)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(-5,-6)$ are the vertices of a rhombus.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "All four sides have length $4\\sqrt5$, so the quadrilateral is a rhombus.",
          "worked": "Label the points $A(3,-2)$, $B(7,6)$, $C(-1,2)$ and $D(-5,-6)$ in the given order.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}AB^2&=(7-3)^2+[6-(-2)]^2\\\\AB^2&=16+64=80\\\\BC^2&=(-1-7)^2+(2-6)^2\\\\BC^2&=64+16=80\\\\CD^2&=[-5-(-1)]^2+(-6-2)^2\\\\CD^2&=16+64=80\\\\DA^2&=[3-(-5)]^2+[-2-(-6)]^2\\\\DA^2&=64+16=80\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $AB=BC=CD=DA=\\sqrt{80}=4\\sqrt5$. The quadrilateral has four equal sides, so it is a rhombus.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the length of each of the diagonals of the rhombus.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$12\\sqrt2$ and $4\\sqrt2$.",
          "worked": "Using the vertex labels from part a, the diagonals are $AC$ and $BD$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}AC^2&=(-1-3)^2+[2-(-2)]^2\\\\AC^2&=16+16=32\\\\AC&=\\sqrt{32}=4\\sqrt2\\\\BD^2&=(-5-7)^2+(-6-6)^2\\\\BD^2&=144+144=288\\\\BD&=\\sqrt{288}=12\\sqrt2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      }
    ]
